Subject: [PATCH] tvm-ffi: the Windows branch is written by halves

THREE DEFECTS, ONE FAMILY, and they are reported together on purpose: each one
is only reachable once the previous is repaired, so three separate reports would
read as three unlucky bugs instead of one branch nobody exercises. Measured on
the Dell (Windows 11, CUDA 12.6, MSVC, RTX 3050) against
apache-tvm-ffi==0.1.13.post3, which DFlash-FreeToken pins; 0.1.14.post1, the
latest at the time of writing, carries the same code.

The Linux branch of the same function is correct in all three places. That is
the point: nobody compiles CUDA kernels through this package on Windows, so the
branch has never been run end to end.

--- 1. `-Xcompiler` takes ONE argument, so /O2 became an input file

    default_cuda_cflags = ["-Xcompiler", "/std:c++17", "/O2"]

nvcc forwards /std:c++17 to cl.exe and is left holding /O2, which it reads as a
SECOND input file. Reproduced on a .cu containing one empty kernel:

    -Xcompiler /std:c++17 /O2   -> nvcc fatal: A single input file is required
                                   for a non-link phase when an outputfile is
                                   specified
    -Xcompiler /std:c++17,/O2   -> argument analysis passes

--- 2. once it compiles, /std:c++17 wins over the caller's /std:c++20

Not fixed here -- DFlash-FreeToken handles it on its own side, and it is named
because it is the same branch and the same mechanism. nvcc puts its own
-std=c++20 on the host line FIRST and every -Xcompiler group AFTER, so tvm-ffi's
/std:c++17 ends up the LAST /std: on cl's line and wins. Headers that need C++20
(<concepts>, <source_location>) then fail with C2039. The caller appends
-Xcompiler /std:c++20,/Zc:__cplusplus, which wins by the same rule --
/Zc:__cplusplus because MSVC otherwise reports __cplusplus as 199711 even when
compiling C++20, and <source_location> hides behind that check.

--- 3. the CUDA runtime library is never linked on Windows

`cudart` appears EXACTLY ONCE in extension.py, at line 444, inside the
non-Windows branch:

    if with_cuda:
        default_ldflags += ["-L{}".format(Path(_find_cuda_home()) / "lib64"),
                            "-lcudart"]

so the Windows link line carries neither the CUDA library path nor the runtime,
and the link dies on the symbols that live in it:

    cuda_0.o : error LNK2019: unresolved external symbol __cudaRegisterFatBinary
    cuda_0.o : error LNK2019: ... __cudaRegisterFatBinaryEnd
    cuda_0.o : error LNK2019: ... __cudaUnregisterFatBinary
    cuda_0.o : error LNK2019: ... __cudaRegisterFunction
    freetoken__index_3072_2_128_1_false.dll : fatal error LNK1120: 8 unresolved

`lib/x64` and not `lib64`: verified on this machine, where
v12.6/lib/x64/cudart.lib exists and v12.6/lib64 does not. `_find_cuda_home()`
resolves correctly from CUDA_PATH.

WHY A VERSIONED PATCH AND NOT AN EDIT IN THE VENV. Editing the installed package
works today and makes this machine differ from a clean install WITHOUT IT BEING
VISIBLE, which is the divergence that would undermine the cross-node
comparability these measurements exist for. Versioned, a clean install rebuilds
the same divergence instead of lacking it. The MacBook Air M2 already carries
patches/llama.cpp-moestream-metrics.patch for the same reason.

--- WHAT THIS PATCH DOES AND DOES NOT TOUCH

Only defect 3. Defect 1 is repaired by `freetoken.kernel.utils` in the engine's
OWN code, and that repair RAISES when it can no longer find the broken string,
because absence means upstream has fixed it. Repairing it here too tripped that
guard -- which did exactly its job. One defect, one fix, in the place that owns
it. Defect 2 is likewise handled engine-side. Both are described above because
a report that shows three instances of the same shape is a stronger argument
than three separate ones.

UPSTREAM: not yet reported. Put the link here when it is, and delete this file
when a released version carries the fix.

--- 4. and the CUDA path has a space in it

Found by fixing 3 and running again, which is how every one of these was found.
The standard CUDA location on Windows is
"C:\Program Files\NVIDIA GPU Computing Toolkit\CUDA\v12.6", and an unquoted
/LIBPATH: is split on the space:

    LINK : fatal error LNK1181: cannot open input file 'Files\NVIDIA.obj'

So the path is quoted here. The neighbouring f"/LIBPATH:{tvm_ffi_lib_path}" is
not, and has never shown it, because that path lives inside the venv where there
is no space -- the same defect waiting for a machine whose user name has one.

WHAT WAS ACTUALLY PROVEN, and the wording matters because it is easy to widen.
With this patch and the engine-side repairs, on the Dell the engine BUILDS ITS
KERNELS, LOADS, AND ANSWERS: 18 tokens, finish_reason=stop, ready 65 s after
launch, 4777 MiB of VRAM. That is "the Windows branch gets as far as generating",
NOT "the Windows branch is repaired" -- each of these four was only reachable
once the previous was fixed, so a fifth may sit further along the path.

--- a/tvm_ffi/cpp/extension.py
+++ b/tvm_ffi/cpp/extension.py
@@ -421,6 +421,15 @@ def _generate_ninja_build(  # noqa: PLR0915, PLR0912
             f"/LIBPATH:{tvm_ffi_lib_path}",
             f"{tvm_ffi_lib_name}.lib",
         ]
+        if with_cuda:
+            default_ldflags += [
+                # QUOTED: the standard CUDA path on Windows contains a space
+                # ("C:\Program Files\NVIDIA GPU Computing Toolkit\..."), and left
+                # unquoted the linker splits it -- LNK1181: cannot open input
+                # file 'Files\NVIDIA.obj'.
+                '/LIBPATH:"{}"'.format(Path(_find_cuda_home()) / "lib" / "x64"),
+                "cudart.lib",
+            ]
     else:
         default_cflags = ["-fPIC", "-O2"]
         default_cxxflags = ["-std=c++17", "-fPIC", "-O2"]
